I really don't think that has been proven.  Yes, it is the popular
mindset here, but it's not something that was ever backed up with
proof.

I personally learned Nethack by making test cases of various
scenarios, and by trying various things with each test case to figure
out which courses of action are better than others.  So basically, I
would go into wizard mode, magic up a test case, then save the test
case so that I could later go back to it.  Thus, I had a folder full
of test cases, which I repeatedly played to learn the game.

Yes, I do think that savescumming in wizard mode is better than not
savescumming in wizard mode, as it can sometimes take a couple minutes
to fully set up a test case.  And considering I only did this in
wizard mode, I clearly wasn't doing this in attempts of deceiving
people into thinking I was playing a legitimate game.

And yes, I do think this method can be faster than just playing the
game normally.  When I first started Slashem, I ascended the first
character I played, specifically because everytime I saw something
that wasn't in vanilla Nethack, I would go into wizard mode, magic up
the new thing, then do the savescumming thing in wizard mode to figure
out the best way to deal with the new thing.


